# the argument of sidgwick-methods-4.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
atom	own_good	my happiness is desirable and a good
atom	other_equal	the equal happiness of any other person is equally desirable
atom	benevolence_rational	disinterested benevolence is, in another sense and independently, rational
atom	egoist_suggestion	when Happiness is spoken of as the sole ultimate good of man, the idea most commonly suggested is that each individual is to seek his own happiness at the expense or to the neglect of that of others
atom	offends	this offends both our sympathetic and our rational regard for others' happiness
atom	aversion_egoistic	Common Sense feels an aversion to the end of Egoistic rather than of Universalistic Hedonism
atom	narrow	one's individual happiness is so narrow and limited, of such necessarily brief duration, and so shifting and insecure while it lasts
atom	lacks_ultimate	one's individual happiness does not possess the characteristics which we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good
atom	individual_unsatisfactory	one's individual happiness is, in many respects, an unsatisfactory mark for one's supreme aim
atom	universal_satisfies	Universal Happiness seems an End that satisfies our imagination by its vastness and sustains our resolution by its comparative security
# S2: "must be equally desirable" read as what Reason shows, not as metaphysical necessity, so it is written without modal operators.
